1
00:00:00,182 --> 00:00:02,733
There are eleven ways to
use Desmos on the SAT,

2
00:00:02,753 --> 00:00:05,020
and most students only use one of them:

3
00:00:05,040 --> 00:00:06,147
arithmetic.

4
00:00:06,397 --> 00:00:08,432
Solve an equation by graphing it.

5
00:00:08,574 --> 00:00:10,682
Click an intersection to solve a system.

6
00:00:10,896 --> 00:00:12,805
Count crossings to count solutions.

7
00:00:12,825 --> 00:00:16,341
And one of them checks an equivalent
expressions question without any algebra at

8
00:00:16,361 --> 00:00:16,947
all.

9
00:00:16,967 --> 00:00:19,288
The first few take seconds to learn.

10
00:00:19,395 --> 00:00:21,733
The equivalent expressions
trick is number nine,

11
00:00:21,753 --> 00:00:25,626
so stay for it, because the right answer
lands exactly on top of the original

12
00:00:25,646 --> 00:00:26,646
graph.

13
00:00:26,896 --> 00:00:29,467
Each trick comes with a
practice question you can try.

14
00:00:29,527 --> 00:00:33,114
And at the end, you'll see
when not to use Desmos,

15
00:00:33,134 --> 00:00:35,813
because sometimes it's slower
than just doing the math.

16
00:00:36,633 --> 00:00:38,349
Eleven questions in all.

17
00:00:39,026 --> 00:00:41,526
Keep score, and see if
you can get all eleven.

18
00:00:41,572 --> 00:00:42,572
Trick one.

19
00:00:42,643 --> 00:00:45,465
Solve any equation by graphing.

20
00:00:45,679 --> 00:00:51,230
Here's a question: What value of x
satisfies three times x minus four,

21
00:00:51,250 --> 00:00:54,215
plus seven, equals two x plus nine?

22
00:00:54,265 --> 00:00:56,943
Pause and try it, with or without Desmos.

23
00:01:00,158 --> 00:01:04,210
In Desmos, type the left side as
y equals three times x minus four,

24
00:01:04,230 --> 00:01:05,230
plus seven.

25
00:01:05,658 --> 00:01:09,245
Then type the right side as
y equals two x plus nine.

26
00:01:09,265 --> 00:01:10,300
Two lines.

27
00:01:10,550 --> 00:01:13,158
The answer is wherever they cross.

28
00:01:13,622 --> 00:01:16,567
You'll need to zoom out to see it,
because they cross pretty far from the

29
00:01:16,587 --> 00:01:17,257
middle.

30
00:01:17,277 --> 00:01:20,364
Click the intersection, and
Desmos shows the point fourteen,

31
00:01:20,384 --> 00:01:21,384
thirty-seven.

32
00:01:21,956 --> 00:01:23,849
The x value is the answer.

33
00:01:24,063 --> 00:01:25,670
x equals fourteen.

34
00:01:25,744 --> 00:01:28,616
You can also type the whole
equation exactly as written,

35
00:01:28,636 --> 00:01:31,601
and Desmos draws a vertical
line at the solution.

36
00:01:31,744 --> 00:01:35,672
Either way, you never had to
distribute or move a single term.

37
00:01:35,725 --> 00:01:36,725
Trick two.

38
00:01:36,904 --> 00:01:38,403
Systems of equations.

39
00:01:39,117 --> 00:01:41,705
If x and y is the solution to the system,

40
00:01:41,725 --> 00:01:46,047
two x plus three y equals
twelve, and x minus y equals one,

41
00:01:46,117 --> 00:01:47,867
what is x plus y?

42
00:01:47,924 --> 00:01:49,031
Pause and try it.

43
00:01:52,277 --> 00:01:55,706
Type both equations into Desmos
exactly as they're written.

44
00:01:56,027 --> 00:01:58,063
You don't need to solve for y first.

45
00:01:58,813 --> 00:02:00,991
Desmos handles that form just fine.

46
00:02:01,043 --> 00:02:04,737
Click where they cross: three, two.

47
00:02:04,757 --> 00:02:07,059
So x is three and y is two.

48
00:02:07,079 --> 00:02:08,344
But read the question again.

49
00:02:08,364 --> 00:02:10,435
It asks for x plus y.

50
00:02:10,757 --> 00:02:13,328
The answer is five, not three.

51
00:02:13,363 --> 00:02:14,363
Trick three.

52
00:02:14,470 --> 00:02:15,899
Counting solutions.

53
00:02:16,720 --> 00:02:19,256
How many solutions does this system have?

54
00:02:19,292 --> 00:02:22,485
y equals x squared minus four x plus one,

55
00:02:22,505 --> 00:02:25,362
and y equals negative two x plus one.

56
00:02:25,436 --> 00:02:26,543
Pause and try it.

57
00:02:29,790 --> 00:02:30,627
Graph both.

58
00:02:30,647 --> 00:02:34,433
Every place the graphs
cross is one solution.

59
00:02:35,433 --> 00:02:38,790
Here, the line cuts through
the parabola in two places.

60
00:02:39,433 --> 00:02:40,933
So there are two solutions.

61
00:02:41,825 --> 00:02:44,218
The answer is C.

62
00:02:44,267 --> 00:02:45,767
Same idea for lines.

63
00:02:46,196 --> 00:02:49,319
If two lines cross once, one solution.

64
00:02:49,339 --> 00:02:51,675
If they're parallel, no solution.

65
00:02:51,695 --> 00:02:53,532
If they land right on top of each other,

66
00:02:53,552 --> 00:02:54,750
infinitely many.

67
00:02:54,770 --> 00:02:55,770
Trick four.

68
00:02:56,235 --> 00:02:58,572
X-intercepts, also called zeros, roots,

69
00:02:58,592 --> 00:02:59,663
or solutions.

70
00:03:00,128 --> 00:03:04,107
What is the sum of the solutions to x
squared minus seven x plus ten equals

71
00:03:04,127 --> 00:03:04,891
zero?

72
00:03:04,911 --> 00:03:06,018
Pause and try it.

73
00:03:09,265 --> 00:03:12,565
Graph y equals x squared
minus seven x plus ten,

74
00:03:12,585 --> 00:03:13,979
and click the curve.

75
00:03:14,979 --> 00:03:17,708
Desmos marks where it crosses the x axis:

76
00:03:17,728 --> 00:03:19,372
two and five.

77
00:03:19,443 --> 00:03:21,621
Add them up, and the answer is seven.

78
00:03:21,689 --> 00:03:23,598
Whenever a question says zeros, roots,

79
00:03:23,618 --> 00:03:25,633
x-intercepts, or solutions of a quadratic,

80
00:03:25,653 --> 00:03:28,975
they all mean the same thing:
where the graph hits the x axis.

81
00:03:29,062 --> 00:03:30,185
Trick five.

82
00:03:30,205 --> 00:03:31,991
Maximums and minimums.

83
00:03:32,170 --> 00:03:35,471
The height of a ball, in feet,
t seconds after it's launched,

84
00:03:35,491 --> 00:03:40,863
is h of t equals negative sixteen t
squared plus sixty-four t plus five.

85
00:03:40,883 --> 00:03:42,312
What's the maximum height?

86
00:03:42,336 --> 00:03:43,443
Pause and try it.

87
00:03:46,690 --> 00:03:50,884
Desmos graphs with x and y, so
type it with x instead of t:

88
00:03:50,904 --> 00:03:56,761
y equals negative sixteen x squared
plus sixty-four x plus five.

89
00:03:57,761 --> 00:04:01,027
Zoom out, because the graph goes way up.

90
00:04:01,047 --> 00:04:02,726
Then click the top of the curve.

91
00:04:04,047 --> 00:04:05,654
Desmos shows two, sixty-nine.

92
00:04:06,455 --> 00:04:07,670
Now be careful.

93
00:04:08,670 --> 00:04:10,741
Two is the time when the ball is highest.

94
00:04:11,098 --> 00:04:12,777
Sixty-nine is the height.

95
00:04:13,597 --> 00:04:17,633
The question asks for height,
so the answer is sixty-nine.

96
00:04:17,716 --> 00:04:18,625
Trick six.

97
00:04:18,645 --> 00:04:19,715
Sliders.

98
00:04:20,109 --> 00:04:22,966
This one handles those questions
with a mystery constant.

99
00:04:23,359 --> 00:04:26,767
In the system k x plus three y equals six,

100
00:04:26,787 --> 00:04:29,966
and two x plus y equals
five, k is a constant.

101
00:04:30,215 --> 00:04:33,288
If the system has no solution, what is k?

102
00:04:33,383 --> 00:04:34,491
Pause and try it.

103
00:04:37,737 --> 00:04:39,594
Type both equations into Desmos.

104
00:04:39,951 --> 00:04:44,380
Because k isn't defined, Desmos
offers to add a slider for it.

105
00:04:44,809 --> 00:04:45,541
Click it.

106
00:04:45,561 --> 00:04:48,739
No solution means the lines
are parallel and never cross.

107
00:04:49,024 --> 00:04:51,668
So drag k until the lines are parallel.

108
00:04:52,168 --> 00:04:54,612
Tip: set the slider to
move in steps of one,

109
00:04:54,632 --> 00:04:56,454
so it lands on whole numbers.

110
00:04:56,525 --> 00:04:59,240
The lines go parallel at k equals six.

111
00:04:59,271 --> 00:05:05,021
To double check, the slope of two x
plus y equals five is negative two.

112
00:05:05,485 --> 00:05:10,894
The slope of k x plus three y
equals six is negative k over three.

113
00:05:10,914 --> 00:05:13,199
Set them equal, and k is six.

114
00:05:15,807 --> 00:05:18,072
Sliders also work for questions like,

115
00:05:18,092 --> 00:05:22,735
for what value of c does this parabola
touch the x axis exactly once?

116
00:05:22,787 --> 00:05:23,787
Trick seven.

117
00:05:24,144 --> 00:05:25,323
Inequalities.

118
00:05:25,679 --> 00:05:31,016
Which point is a solution to the system
y is greater than two x minus three,

119
00:05:31,036 --> 00:05:34,573
and y is less than or equal
to negative x plus six?

120
00:05:34,616 --> 00:05:35,723
Pause and try it.

121
00:05:38,970 --> 00:05:40,649
Type both inequalities.

122
00:05:40,899 --> 00:05:45,649
Desmos shades each one, and the solutions
are wherever the shading overlaps.

123
00:05:45,899 --> 00:05:49,434
The dashed line means
greater than, not equal to,

124
00:05:49,470 --> 00:05:51,684
so points on that line don't count.

125
00:05:51,730 --> 00:05:53,873
Now type the four points from the choices.

126
00:05:54,373 --> 00:05:58,337
Only one lands inside the overlap: one,

127
00:05:58,587 --> 00:05:59,587
two.

128
00:05:59,730 --> 00:06:01,552
The answer is C.

129
00:06:01,621 --> 00:06:02,621
Trick eight.

130
00:06:03,014 --> 00:06:04,193
Regression.

131
00:06:04,693 --> 00:06:07,550
This is the most underrated Desmos move.

132
00:06:07,693 --> 00:06:10,672
The table shows three points
from a linear relationship:

133
00:06:10,692 --> 00:06:15,477
one and seven, three and
thirteen, six and twenty-two.

134
00:06:15,585 --> 00:06:17,157
Which equation represents it?

135
00:06:17,218 --> 00:06:18,325
Pause and try it.

136
00:06:21,572 --> 00:06:24,465
In Desmos, add a table
and type the points in.

137
00:06:25,358 --> 00:06:29,194
Then, on a new line, type y
one, then the tilde symbol,

138
00:06:29,214 --> 00:06:32,607
the little squiggle, then m x one plus b.

139
00:06:34,536 --> 00:06:37,143
Desmos finds the line
that fits the points.

140
00:06:37,170 --> 00:06:40,419
It gives m equals three and b equals four.

141
00:06:40,597 --> 00:06:43,364
So the answer is y
equals three x plus four,

142
00:06:43,384 --> 00:06:44,526
choice A.

143
00:06:44,848 --> 00:06:48,899
Notice B and C both work for the
first point but not the others.

144
00:06:48,919 --> 00:06:51,670
That's exactly the trap
regression protects you from.

145
00:06:51,752 --> 00:06:57,323
For exponential tables, use y
one tilde a times b to the x one.

146
00:06:57,787 --> 00:06:58,787
Same idea.

147
00:06:58,885 --> 00:06:59,900
Trick nine.

148
00:06:59,920 --> 00:07:01,313
Equivalent expressions.

149
00:07:01,812 --> 00:07:04,492
Which expression is
equivalent to x plus three,

150
00:07:04,563 --> 00:07:07,277
squared, minus two times x plus three?

151
00:07:07,315 --> 00:07:08,422
Pause and try it.

152
00:07:11,668 --> 00:07:13,989
Graph the original expression as y equals.

153
00:07:14,275 --> 00:07:16,704
Then graph the choices one at a time.

154
00:07:17,240 --> 00:07:19,863
Equivalent expressions
make the exact same graph,

155
00:07:19,883 --> 00:07:22,740
so the right answer will sit
perfectly on top of the original.

156
00:07:22,808 --> 00:07:24,844
Choice A lands right on top.

157
00:07:25,023 --> 00:07:28,558
The answer is x squared
plus four x plus three.

158
00:07:28,612 --> 00:07:29,612
Trick ten.

159
00:07:30,255 --> 00:07:31,325
Functions.

160
00:07:32,075 --> 00:07:35,092
If f of x equals two
x squared minus three,

161
00:07:35,112 --> 00:07:40,147
and g of x equals x plus four,
what is f of g of negative one?

162
00:07:40,184 --> 00:07:41,291
Pause and try it.

163
00:07:44,537 --> 00:07:47,232
Desmos understands function notation.

164
00:07:47,252 --> 00:07:50,466
Type f of x equals two
x squared minus three.

165
00:07:50,716 --> 00:07:53,216
Type g of x equals x plus four.

166
00:07:53,716 --> 00:07:58,195
Then type f of g of negative
one, and Desmos just tells you:

167
00:07:58,215 --> 00:07:59,216
fifteen.

168
00:07:59,265 --> 00:08:04,888
By hand, g of negative one is three,
and f of three is eighteen minus three,

169
00:08:04,908 --> 00:08:06,194
which is fifteen.

170
00:08:06,337 --> 00:08:10,443
Same answer, but Desmos makes it
nearly impossible to slip on the signs.

171
00:08:10,470 --> 00:08:11,470
Trick eleven.

172
00:08:12,541 --> 00:08:13,612
Statistics.

173
00:08:14,113 --> 00:08:16,807
For the data set four, eight, eight, ten,

174
00:08:16,827 --> 00:08:20,792
fifteen, twenty-one, what is
the mean minus the median?

175
00:08:20,857 --> 00:08:21,964
Pause and try it.

176
00:08:25,211 --> 00:08:29,247
Type L equals, then the
numbers inside square brackets.

177
00:08:29,711 --> 00:08:31,247
Now L is a list.

178
00:08:31,925 --> 00:08:36,317
Type mean of L minus median of
L, and Desmos gives you two.

179
00:08:36,360 --> 00:08:39,073
The mean is eleven and the median is nine.

180
00:08:40,717 --> 00:08:44,252
Desmos also has functions for
things like standard deviation,

181
00:08:44,394 --> 00:08:48,359
but on the SAT you'll usually just compare
spreads instead of calculating them.

182
00:08:48,430 --> 00:08:51,001
Now, when shouldn't you use Desmos?

183
00:08:51,322 --> 00:08:53,858
First, word problems.

184
00:08:54,430 --> 00:08:56,287
Desmos can't read.

185
00:08:56,536 --> 00:08:58,767
You still have to turn the
words into an equation,

186
00:08:58,787 --> 00:09:00,429
and that's usually the hard part.

187
00:09:00,496 --> 00:09:03,568
Second, quick arithmetic
and one-step algebra.

188
00:09:03,888 --> 00:09:06,210
If you can do it in your
head in five seconds,

189
00:09:06,389 --> 00:09:08,031
opening Desmos is slower.

190
00:09:08,118 --> 00:09:10,296
Third, watch the window.

191
00:09:10,332 --> 00:09:12,705
If you don't see an
intersection or a vertex,

192
00:09:12,725 --> 00:09:14,903
zoom out before you decide it isn't there.

193
00:09:14,936 --> 00:09:16,559
And one bonus tip.

194
00:09:16,579 --> 00:09:19,595
If the answer choices have
square roots or pi in them,

195
00:09:19,615 --> 00:09:22,972
type each choice into Desmos
to turn it into a decimal,

196
00:09:23,042 --> 00:09:24,793
then compare it to your answer.

197
00:09:24,845 --> 00:09:25,917
Quick recap.

198
00:09:26,453 --> 00:09:28,345
Solve equations by graphing.

199
00:09:28,774 --> 00:09:30,524
Click intersections for systems.

200
00:09:30,774 --> 00:09:31,988
Count crossings.

201
00:09:32,487 --> 00:09:34,237
Click intercepts and vertices.

202
00:09:34,737 --> 00:09:36,810
Use sliders for mystery constants.

203
00:09:37,345 --> 00:09:39,131
Test points against inequalities.

204
00:09:39,667 --> 00:09:41,560
Run regressions on tables.

205
00:09:42,203 --> 00:09:44,290
Graph expressions to check
if they're equivalent.

206
00:09:44,310 --> 00:09:45,810
Evaluate functions.

207
00:09:46,166 --> 00:09:48,167
And use lists for statistics.

208
00:09:48,205 --> 00:09:51,721
The best way to get fast is to practice
with the same calculator you'll use on

209
00:09:51,741 --> 00:09:52,721
test day.

210
00:09:52,741 --> 00:09:54,919
Bluebook's practice
tests have it built in.

211
00:09:54,966 --> 00:09:57,517
Every practice question here
was written for this video,

212
00:09:57,537 --> 00:09:59,252
in the style of the real test.

213
00:09:59,466 --> 00:10:01,395
If this helped, subscribe.

214
00:10:02,038 --> 00:10:06,375
The next video is ten hard SAT math
questions to try before the November seventh

215
00:10:06,395 --> 00:10:07,268
test.

216
00:10:07,288 --> 00:10:08,288
Good luck.
